#: ../../.opencode/skills/dependency-analysis/SKILL.md:9
msgid "What I do"
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/dependency-analysis/SKILL.md:11
msgid ""
"Provide a systematic framework for evaluating software dependencies. "
"Every library you depend on is code you don't control — this skill helps "
"you assess whether that trade-off is worth it and when it stops being "
"worth it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/dependency-analysis/SKILL.md:13
msgid "When to use me"
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/dependency-analysis/SKILL.md:15
msgid ""
"Load this skill when evaluating a new dependency for adoption, auditing "
"existing dependencies for risk, or deciding whether to replace a library "
"with an alternative."
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/dependency-analysis/SKILL.md:17
msgid "Health signals"
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/dependency-analysis/SKILL.md:19
msgid ""
"A library's health predicts whether it will receive bug fixes, security "
"patches, and compatibility updates. Evaluate these signals together — no "
"single metric is conclusive."
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/dependency-analysis/SKILL.md:21
msgid "Commit frequency"
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/dependency-analysis/SKILL.md:23
msgid ""
"**Healthy**: Regular commits (weekly to monthly) over the past year. "
"Doesn't need to be daily — mature libraries often have lower commit "
"frequency."
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/dependency-analysis/SKILL.md:24
msgid ""
"**Warning**: No commits in 6+ months on a library that's not \"finished\""
" (i.e., it interacts with changing ecosystems like web frameworks, "
"language runtimes, or APIs)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/dependency-analysis/SKILL.md:25
msgid ""
"**Exception**: Some libraries are genuinely complete (e.g., a math "
"utility). No commits is fine if there are no open bugs and the library's "
"domain is stable."
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/dependency-analysis/SKILL.md:27
msgid "Issue response time"
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/dependency-analysis/SKILL.md:29
msgid ""
"Check the last 20 issues. How quickly does a maintainer respond? A "
"response within a week is healthy. Issues sitting unanswered for months "
"is a warning."
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/dependency-analysis/SKILL.md:30
msgid ""
"Distinguish between \"acknowledged and triaged\" (healthy) and \"no "
"response at all\" (warning). Not every issue needs an immediate fix, but "
"silence signals abandonment."
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/dependency-analysis/SKILL.md:32
msgid "Release cadence"
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/dependency-analysis/SKILL.md:34
msgid "Regular releases (even if infrequent) indicate active maintenance."
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/dependency-analysis/SKILL.md:35
msgid ""
"A long gap between the last commit and the last release means fixes exist"
" but aren't being shipped. This is sometimes worse than no commits — "
"users can't get patches without building from source."
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/dependency-analysis/SKILL.md:37
msgid "Bus factor"
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/dependency-analysis/SKILL.md:39
msgid ""
"How many people have committed in the past year? A library maintained by "
"a single person is a risk — if that person stops, the library stops."
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/dependency-analysis/SKILL.md:40
msgid ""
"Check if the library is backed by an organization or company. "
"Organizational backing isn't a guarantee, but it reduces single-point-of-"
"failure risk."
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/dependency-analysis/SKILL.md:41
msgid ""
"Check if there are documented maintainer succession plans or multiple "
"people with release permissions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/dependency-analysis/SKILL.md:43
msgid "Community signals"
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/dependency-analysis/SKILL.md:45
msgid "Stars and download counts indicate adoption but not quality."
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/dependency-analysis/SKILL.md:46
msgid ""
"More useful: Are there active forks? Are PRs from external contributors "
"being merged? Is there a discussion forum or Discord with actual "
"activity?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/dependency-analysis/SKILL.md:48
msgid "Vulnerability assessment"
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/dependency-analysis/SKILL.md:50
msgid "CVE databases"
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/dependency-analysis/SKILL.md:52
msgid ""
"Check the library against the National Vulnerability Database (NVD), "
"GitHub Advisory Database, and language-specific advisory databases (e.g.,"
" RustSec for Rust, npm advisories for JavaScript)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/dependency-analysis/SKILL.md:53
msgid ""
"Look at not just current vulnerabilities but the history. A library with "
"frequent critical CVEs may have systemic security problems in its design,"
" not just individual bugs."
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/dependency-analysis/SKILL.md:55
msgid "Dependency tree depth"
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/dependency-analysis/SKILL.md:57
msgid ""
"A library with 3 dependencies is easier to audit than one with 300. Deep "
"dependency trees increase the surface area for vulnerabilities."
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/dependency-analysis/SKILL.md:58
msgid ""
"Map the transitive dependency tree. You're responsible for "
"vulnerabilities in every transitive dependency, even if you didn't choose"
" them directly."
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/dependency-analysis/SKILL.md:59
msgid ""
"Watch for duplicate dependencies at different versions — this can "
"indicate incompatibility issues and increases attack surface."
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/dependency-analysis/SKILL.md:61
msgid "Transitive risks"
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/dependency-analysis/SKILL.md:63
msgid ""
"A direct dependency may be healthy, but its dependencies may not be. "
"Check the health signals of significant transitive dependencies "
"(especially those that handle network, crypto, or parsing)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/dependency-analysis/SKILL.md:64
msgid ""
"Use tools like `npm audit`, `cargo audit`, `pip audit`, or `go vuln "
"check` to scan the full tree."
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/dependency-analysis/SKILL.md:66
msgid "License compatibility"
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/dependency-analysis/SKILL.md:68
msgid "Copyleft vs permissive"
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/dependency-analysis/SKILL.md:70
msgid ""
"**Permissive** (MIT, Apache 2.0, BSD): Use freely in commercial and open-"
"source projects. Minimal obligations (typically attribution only)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/dependency-analysis/SKILL.md:71
msgid ""
"**Weak copyleft** (LGPL, MPL): Can be used in proprietary software if the"
" library itself remains open and modifications to it are shared. Dynamic "
"linking is generally safe; static linking may trigger copyleft "
"obligations."
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/dependency-analysis/SKILL.md:72
msgid ""
"**Strong copyleft** (GPL, AGPL): Requires derivative works to be released"
" under the same license. Using a GPL library in a proprietary project "
"typically means the entire project must be GPL. AGPL extends this to "
"network use — even SaaS deployments trigger the obligation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/dependency-analysis/SKILL.md:74
msgid "Commercial use restrictions"
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/dependency-analysis/SKILL.md:76
msgid ""
"Some licenses include non-commercial clauses (CC-BY-NC) or field-of-use "
"restrictions. These are incompatible with most commercial projects."
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/dependency-analysis/SKILL.md:77
msgid ""
"Check for \"dual licensing\" where the open-source license is restrictive"
" but a commercial license is available for purchase."
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/dependency-analysis/SKILL.md:78
msgid ""
"Check for license changes between versions. Some libraries have changed "
"from permissive to restrictive licenses in recent major versions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/dependency-analysis/SKILL.md:80
msgid "License compliance checklist"
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/dependency-analysis/SKILL.md:82
msgid "Read the actual license text, not just the SPDX identifier"
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/dependency-analysis/SKILL.md:83
msgid "Verify all transitive dependencies have compatible licenses"
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/dependency-analysis/SKILL.md:84
msgid "Check for NOTICE files or additional terms beyond the license"
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/dependency-analysis/SKILL.md:85
msgid "Document the license in your project's dependency manifest"
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/dependency-analysis/SKILL.md:86
msgid ""
"Set up automated license scanning in CI to catch new incompatible "
"dependencies"
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/dependency-analysis/SKILL.md:88
msgid "Size and bloat"
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/dependency-analysis/SKILL.md:90
msgid "Bundle size impact"
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/dependency-analysis/SKILL.md:92
msgid "For frontend dependencies:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/dependency-analysis/SKILL.md:93
msgid ""
"Check the minified + gzipped size. Use tools like bundlephobia.com or "
"`import-cost` editor plugins."
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/dependency-analysis/SKILL.md:94
msgid ""
"Compare the size to the functionality you're actually using. If you need "
"one utility function from a 50KB library, consider copying the function "
"or finding a smaller alternative."
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/dependency-analysis/SKILL.md:96
msgid "Tree-shaking compatibility"
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/dependency-analysis/SKILL.md:98
msgid ""
"Libraries that export ESM modules and have proper `sideEffects: false` "
"configuration can be tree-shaken — unused exports are removed from the "
"bundle."
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/dependency-analysis/SKILL.md:99
msgid ""
"Libraries that use CommonJS, have side effects in module initialization, "
"or export a single large object cannot be tree-shaken."
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/dependency-analysis/SKILL.md:100
msgid ""
"Check the library's `package.json` for `module`, `exports`, and "
"`sideEffects` fields."
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/dependency-analysis/SKILL.md:102
msgid "Runtime impact"
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/dependency-analysis/SKILL.md:104
msgid ""
"Some libraries add significant runtime overhead (heavy initialization, "
"frequent GC pressure, large memory footprint). Profile before adopting in"
" performance-sensitive paths."
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/dependency-analysis/SKILL.md:105
msgid ""
"Check if the library spawns threads, opens connections, or allocates "
"large buffers on import."
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/dependency-analysis/SKILL.md:107
msgid "Alternative evaluation"
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/dependency-analysis/SKILL.md:109
msgid "When to switch"
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/dependency-analysis/SKILL.md:111
msgid "Consider switching when:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/dependency-analysis/SKILL.md:112
msgid ""
"The current library is unmaintained and has known vulnerabilities with no"
" fix"
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/dependency-analysis/SKILL.md:113
msgid "The current library's license has changed to something incompatible"
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/dependency-analysis/SKILL.md:114
msgid ""
"A clearly superior alternative exists that the team has bandwidth to "
"migrate to"
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/dependency-analysis/SKILL.md:115
msgid ""
"The current library is the source of recurring bugs or performance "
"problems"
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/dependency-analysis/SKILL.md:116
msgid ""
"The current library doesn't support a required platform, runtime, or "
"language version"
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/dependency-analysis/SKILL.md:118
msgid "When NOT to switch"
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/dependency-analysis/SKILL.md:120
msgid ""
"The current library works fine and the alternative is just \"newer\" or "
"\"more popular\""
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/dependency-analysis/SKILL.md:121
msgid ""
"The migration cost exceeds the benefit over the remaining lifetime of the"
" project"
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/dependency-analysis/SKILL.md:122
msgid ""
"The alternative is less mature — early adoption of shiny new libraries is"
" its own risk"
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/dependency-analysis/SKILL.md:124
msgid "Migration cost assessment"
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/dependency-analysis/SKILL.md:126
msgid "Before recommending a switch, estimate:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/dependency-analysis/SKILL.md:127
msgid "How many call sites use the current library? (Search the codebase.)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/dependency-analysis/SKILL.md:128
msgid ""
"Are the APIs similar enough for mechanical replacement, or does each call"
" site need manual attention?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/dependency-analysis/SKILL.md:129
msgid ""
"Does the new library handle all the edge cases the current one handles? "
"(Check test coverage.)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/dependency-analysis/SKILL.md:130
msgid ""
"What's the testing cost? Every integration with the new library needs "
"verification."
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/dependency-analysis/SKILL.md:131
msgid ""
"Can the migration be done incrementally with an adapter pattern, or is it"
" all-or-nothing?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/dependency-analysis/SKILL.md:133
msgid "Supply chain security"
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/dependency-analysis/SKILL.md:135
msgid "Lockfile integrity"
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/dependency-analysis/SKILL.md:137
msgid ""
"Always commit lockfiles (`package-lock.json`, `Cargo.lock`, `go.sum`, "
"`poetry.lock`). Without a lockfile, builds are non-reproducible and "
"vulnerable to dependency confusion."
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/dependency-analysis/SKILL.md:138
msgid ""
"Review lockfile changes in PRs. A lockfile diff that adds unexpected "
"dependencies or changes versions you didn't intend is a signal to "
"investigate."
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/dependency-analysis/SKILL.md:140
msgid "Provenance"
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/dependency-analysis/SKILL.md:142
msgid ""
"Verify that published packages match their source code. Some registries "
"support provenance attestation (npm with Sigstore, Python with PEP 740)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/dependency-analysis/SKILL.md:143
msgid ""
"Prefer packages published from CI (with provenance) over those published "
"from a developer's local machine."
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/dependency-analysis/SKILL.md:145
msgid "Typosquatting risks"
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/dependency-analysis/SKILL.md:147
msgid ""
"Before adopting a new package, verify the name is correct. `lodash` vs "
"`1odash`, `requests` vs `request` — typosquatting is a real attack "
"vector."
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/dependency-analysis/SKILL.md:148
msgid ""
"Check the package's author, repository link, and download count. A "
"package with the right name but 50 downloads and no linked repository is "
"suspicious."
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/dependency-analysis/SKILL.md:149
msgid ""
"Use tools that detect known typosquatting packages in your dependency "
"tree."
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/dependency-analysis/SKILL.md:151
msgid "Evaluation summary format"
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/dependency-analysis/SKILL.md:153
msgid "When reporting on a dependency evaluation, structure the findings as:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/dependency-analysis/SKILL.md:155
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"## {Library name} v{version}\n"
"\n"
"**Recommendation**: Adopt / Keep / Replace / Remove\n"
"**Risk level**: Low / Medium / High\n"
"\n"
"### Health\n"
"- Maintainers: {count}, last activity: {date}\n"
"- Release cadence: {description}\n"
"- Open issues: {count}, avg response time: {duration}\n"
"\n"
"### Security\n"
"- Known CVEs: {count}, unpatched: {count}\n"
"- Dependency tree depth: {count} direct, {count} transitive\n"
"- Last audit: {date}\n"
"\n"
"### License\n"
"- License: {SPDX identifier}\n"
"- Compatible with project: Yes / No / Requires review\n"
"- Transitive license risks: {description}\n"
"\n"
"### Size\n"
"- Bundle size: {size} min+gzip\n"
"- Tree-shakeable: Yes / No\n"
"\n"
"### Alternatives considered\n"
"- {Alternative 1}: {one-line comparison}\n"
"- {Alternative 2}: {one-line comparison}\n"
"\n"
"### Migration cost (if recommending replacement)\n"
"- Estimated effort: {description}\n"
"- Call sites to update: {count}\n"
"- Incremental migration possible: Yes / No\n"
msgstr ""

